WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:05.180
Fittingly for a show about the future, Futurama
was ahead of the curve in all sorts of ways.

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:09.220
Who would have thought that a world full of
alcohol-powered robots and Smell-O-Scopes

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:10.740
would end up resembling our own?

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.640
Here's all the times that Futurama freakishly
predicted the future.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.700
One of the most ubiquitous pieces of technology
in the world of Futurama is the wrist-mounted

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:21.200
computer.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.640
Though they never get an official name, Leela
typically refers to hers as a "wrist thingy."

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.920
Leela has used her wrist thingy as a phone,
a clock, a remote control for the Planet Express

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.989
ship, and basically everything else.

00:00:32.989 --> 00:00:37.120
Ever since the days of The Jetsons, we have
longed for wristwatch computers, but when

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.760
Futurama premiered, they were still a distant
dream.

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:46.200
The closest thing we had at the time was Samsung's
SPH-WP10, a wrist-mounted cell phone that

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:49.100
weighed 50 grams and had a battery life of
90 minutes.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.719
Over the past few years though, this dream
has more or less come true, with smartwatches

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.019
like the Apple Watch that can now approximate
many of the functions of a smart phone.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.299
Of course, unlike Leela's wrist thingy, the
Apple Watch can't record someone rapping and

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.020
then analyze the success rate of their rhyme-busting.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.700
In many ways, our modern world doesn't look
all that different from 1999 when Futurama

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:11.500
first premiered.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.620
Most of our technological progress in the
past two decades has been in the realm of

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.120
computers, rather than jetpacks and flying
cars.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:22.960
However, we have had one major change to the
skies of our cities, and that's the development

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.660
of quadcopter drones.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:30.009
In recent years, technology surrounding unmanned
aerial vehicles has progressed by leaps and

00:01:30.009 --> 00:01:34.670
bounds, leading to widespread availability
of cheap quadcopter drones for pretty much

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:35.950
anyone who wants one.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.520
Believe it or not, Futurama kind of called
this one.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:43.540
Floating camera drones appear all throughout
Futurama in lieu of human camera operators.

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:48.390
However, no one in the Futurama-verse figured
out that drones can be used to make deliveries.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:52.520
It's a good thing, too, or else the Planet
Express crew might be out of a job.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:58.100
In the episode "The Lesser of Two Evils,"
Fry, Leela, and Bender visit Past-o-Rama,

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:02.900
a theme park that is supposed to recreate
late 20th/early 21st century New York.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.020
It's not exactly accurate.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.619
The only clearly predictive element of scenery
is the marquee at the theater, which advertises

00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:13.110
"Star Wars 9 — Yoda's Bar Mitzvah."

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:16.959
When "The Lesser of Two Evils" first aired,
the newest Star Wars film to come out was

00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:21.760
Star Wars: Episode I – The Phantom Menace,
and given how poorly received that film was,

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:25.180
the thought that we'd eventually get an Episode
IX was far from certain.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:30.280
This isn't the only time that one of Futurama's
joke movies ended up actually getting made.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.849
In the episode "A Fishful of Dollars," Fry
meets the head of Pamela Anderson, who tells

00:02:34.849 --> 00:02:36.880
him that she starred in "Baywatch: The Movie."

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:37.960
"Hello Fry.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.240
Remember me from Baywatch the movie?”

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:41.240
“Uhh….”

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.980
“It was the first movie to be shot entirely
in slow-motion."

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.420
18 years later, a theatrical Baywatch film
did hit theaters, with Anderson providing

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:50.940
a cameo.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:56.120
In the world of Futurama, robots like Bender
are powered by alcohol.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:01.080
Out of all the wacky elements from Futurama's
setting that might actually come to pass,

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:05.820
there's no way that we're going to actually
get machines that run on beer, will we?

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.920
We present to you the newest innovation in
automotive fuel: beer.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:15.980
Created by New Zealand-based brewery DB Export,
Brewtroleum is an ethanol fuel made from the

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:19.680
yeast and grain waste products left over by
the brewing process.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:24.920
Okay, so it's not actually beer, but finding
leftover bits of biomass from other industries

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:29.740
and processing those waste products into ethanol
fuel could be the future of biofuels.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:34.000
It's frequently less work, and according to
some reports, growing crops explicitly for

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.620
use as biofuels could contribute to food shortages.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:42.659
So if you want the full Futurama experience
of sharing a beer with one of your robot friends,

00:03:42.659 --> 00:03:44.989
it turns out that finding the fuel is easy.

00:03:44.989 --> 00:03:47.400
The hard part is finding a robot that runs
on diesel.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:53.299
You could always try the BigDog robot that
was developed by Boston Dynamics back in 2005,

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.020
which does have a gas-powered engine.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:59.180
Just don't make the mistake of sharing a beer
with your flesh-and-blood dog, too.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.720
Even though Futurama takes place in the 31st
century, most of the prominent celebrities

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.209
in that world are the same ones we have today.

00:04:07.209 --> 00:04:11.489
However, in most cases, all that's left of
them is their heads, which are kept alive

00:04:11.489 --> 00:04:12.820
in jars full of liquid.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:17.460
We might not have cracked the science of preserving
heads in jars yet in our century, but through

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:21.810
new advances in computer-generated imagery,
we are fast approaching a world where celebrities

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:22.520
never die.

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:27.080
Ever since the debut of the infamous Tupac
"hologram," the genie has been out of the

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:30.920
bottle in terms of using technology to virtually
resurrect the dead.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:35.880
In Star Wars: Rogue One, motion capture and
CGI meant that the late Peter Cushing could

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:41.140
reprise his role as Grand Moff Tarkin, and
a digitally de-aged Carrie Fisher could make

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:42.300
a cameo as Leia.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.780
After Paul Walker's unexpected death during
the production of Furious 7, a mixture of

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:51.120
visual effects techniques recreated his likeness
for the last few scenes he was unavailable

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:51.620
to film.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:57.140
An especially controversial recent example
is the virtual casting of James Dean in the

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:58.360
film Finding Jack.

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:02.260
Say what you want about preserving a head
in a jar — at least in that case the dead

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:04.120
celebrity can voice an opinion.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:08.920
Futurama wasn't the first work of science
fiction to posit using computers to create

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:11.190
immersive simulated experiences.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:16.950
Novels like Neuromancer and films like Brainstorm
told stories about virtual reality decades

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:17.440
earlier.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.000
However, Futurama seems to be the first work
of fiction to predict how quickly this technology

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.200
would be used to recreate and gamify totally
mundane experiences.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:31.320
Futurama first presented its take on VR in
the episode "The Series Has Landed."

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:35.771
When the Planet Express crew visits an amusement
park on the moon to deliver a package, they

00:05:35.771 --> 00:05:38.400
swing by a video arcade to play some games.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.460
First, we see someone playing Skeeball.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:43.080
Then, we see someone playing Virtual Skeeball.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:48.320
And finally, we see Amy playing Virtual Virtual
Skeeball, wearing a VR headset and just sitting

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:49.000
in a chair.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:54.780
A couple decades later, a pair of games shockingly
similar to Virtual Virtual Skeeball come along

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:58.940
in the form of Job Simulator and its sequel,
Vacation Simulator.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:03.960
These comedic games use the magic of Oculus
Rift technology to create immersive simulations

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:06.340
of completely mundane situations.

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:11.640
"Hello human, welcome to an accurate simulation
of office worker."

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:15.690
These sorts of tongue-in-cheek games obviously
aren't the only thing that virtual reality

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:20.400
is being used for, but they do speak to a
larger cultural trend that Futurama predicted

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.780
— no technology, no matter how awe-inspiring,
can keep blowing our minds forever.

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:27.200
Eventually, everything becomes a punchline.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:32.430
Professor Farnsworth has created a lot of
iconic inventions over the years, such as

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:36.420
the Coolometer, the What-If Machine, and the
Fing-Longer.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:41.510
Perhaps his most memorable is the Smell-O-Scope,
which allows its users to smell objects at

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:43.140
an astronomical scale.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:44.800
As Farnsworth puts it:

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.180
"That's my prize-winning smell-o-scope.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:52.460
If a dog craps anywhere in the universe, you
can bet I won't be out of the loop."

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:57.800
As silly as this device may be, it's not too
far off from a real-world invention known

00:06:57.810 --> 00:06:59.670
as the Nasal Ranger.

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:05.120
Unfortunately, the Nasal Ranger can't smell
objects at long ranges — if you aren't in

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.540
the same space as a given odor, with actual
bits of it entering your nose, there's no

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:10.460
way to smell it.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:15.820
However, this "field olfactometer" can be
used by a trained operator to quantify just

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:17.790
how strong a smell really is.

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:23.961
The Nasal Ranger allows its user to inhale
ambient air filtered to varying degrees, as

00:07:23.961 --> 00:07:26.160
determined by an adjustable dial.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:31.310
By changing the tolerance of the filters,
an operator can discover just how much a stench

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:33.900
needs to be diluted before it is no longer
offensive.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:38.480
According to a segment of Modern Marvels all
about the Nasal Ranger, any scent that can

00:07:38.481 --> 00:07:43.310
still be detected when one part of it is diluted
with 15 parts clean air is "a nuisance."

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:48.070
As cool as the Nasal Ranger is, we're a little
disappointed that its creators opted to measure

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:52.890
stinkiness with dilution ratios, instead of
the Smell-O-Scope's far more scientifically

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:54.640
exact "Funkometer."

00:07:55.240 --> 00:08:01.280
In "I Dated a Robot," Fry discovers a service
that sells robotic companions with the personalities

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:03.040
and appearances of celebrities.

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:08.440
But shortly after Fry purchases one based
on Lucy Liu, circa 2003, he learns a terrible

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:08.940
truth.

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:13.760
This company is programming its robots by
kidnapping real celebrity heads and forcefully

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:15.160
scanning their brains.

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:20.140
In the end, Fry rescues the real Lucy Liu
and the other kidnapped celebrity heads.

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:25.500
Finally, he destroys his unlawfully acquired
Liu-bot, in keeping with actual Lucy Liu's

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:26.000
wishes.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:30.240
Though realistic robots are still a ways off,
we're getting closer every year.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:35.020
One company recently released a version of
a doll with a customizable robotic head capable

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:36.480
of speech and limited movement.

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:41.969
As a joke, comedian Whitney Cummings purchased
one customized to look like herself, and incorporated

00:08:41.969 --> 00:08:43.840
the robot into a stand-up special.

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:49.050
Perhaps predictably, shortly thereafter the
company Realbotix reported a deluge of requests

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:51.440
from customers for Whitney Cummings robots.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:56.640
However, Realbotix rejected these requests,
since they didn't own Cummings' likeness.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:01.779
Another form of entertainment this episode
unintentionally predicts is deepfake adult

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:02.320
entertainment.

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:06.780
The latest trend on many adult streaming sites
are videos that have been edited with facial

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:12.079
replacement technology to impose celebrities'
faces onto the bodies of adult actors without

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.080
the consent of the given celebrities.

00:09:14.949 --> 00:09:19.960
When the crew goes to watch "All My Circuits:
The Movie" in the episode "Raging Bender,"

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:22.780
we see that movies have changed a bit since
the 21st century.

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:27.770
As robot businessman Calculon is finishing
some paperwork, he receives a phone call alerting

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:31.579
him that a fight scene has broken out at the
special effects warehouse.

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:34.600
An announcer asks the audience to choose what
happens next:

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:40.180
"If you want Calculon to race to the special
effects battle, press 1.

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:43.740
If you want Calculon to doublecheck his paperwork,
press 2.”

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:49.060
The audience selects "2," and Calculon spends
the next few minutes sitting at his desk,

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:50.260
flipping through papers.

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:54.600
The promise of interactive films has been
on the frontier of cinema for decades.

00:09:54.600 --> 00:10:00.220
The first interactive movie was the 1967 Czech
film Kinoautomat, but surprisingly few films

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:02.460
have followed in Kinoautomat's tracks.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:06.340
Since the premiere of "Raging Bender," much
of the hype around interactive films has been

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:11.420
realized in narrative-driven video games like
Her Story, The Stanley Parable, or the work

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:12.580
of Telltale Games.

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:17.050
Black Mirror recently experimented with the
potential of streaming services to create

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:20.640
interactive fiction with its mind-bending
special "Bandersnatch."

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:25.550
It was, like many interactive movies, praised
for its novelty, but criticized for not having

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:27.480
much depth beyond its high concept.

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:32.920
One instance of Futurama's uncanny prescience
came in an episode that ended up predicting

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:35.600
not a technological trend, but a news story.

00:10:35.949 --> 00:10:40.400
In "The Lesser of Two Evils," Leela, Fry,
and Bender find themselves at the Miss Universe

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:40.940
pageant.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:46.860
As the crew is watching the show, one of Leela's
exes, Zapp Brannigan, is presenting the award.

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:51.640
When Zapp notices Leela as he opens the envelope,
he calls out her name in surprise.

00:10:51.649 --> 00:10:56.941
However, the pageant staff misinterprets this
as Zapp announcing Leela as the winner, so

00:10:56.941 --> 00:11:00.029
they drag her on stage and give her flowers
and a crown.

00:11:00.029 --> 00:11:03.959
Moments later, Zapp corrects the error, and
Leela is stripped of her crown and flowers

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:05.800
to be given to the actual winner.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:10.080
In 2015, the actual Miss Universe pageant
had a mix-up of its own.

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:14.920
Moments after Miss Colombia was crowned Miss
Universe, comedian Steve Harvey, who was in

00:11:14.939 --> 00:11:18.870
charge of presenting the winner, realized
that he had misread the piece of paper he

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:21.560
was given and accidentally named the wrong
winner.

00:11:21.889 --> 00:11:25.779
When he started to explain, the audience thought
he was kidding around at first, but he clarified

00:11:25.779 --> 00:11:27.160
that this was no joke.

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:29.170
The actual winner was Miss Philippines.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:34.640
It was a fiasco worthy of an animated sitcom,
but given that this was happening in the real

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:37.520
world, everyone involved found it a whole
lot less funny.

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:40.000
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:43.440
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
shows are coming soon.

